1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,730
KAIYU YANG: And, hey, it's
great to see you here.

2
00:00:02,730 --> 00:00:06,860
I'm Kaiyu, and I will talk
about Formal Reasoning Meets

3
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:11,750
LLMs: Towards AI for
Mathematics and Verification.

4
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:16,580
OK, so we are now kind of in
an arms race of large language

5
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:22,190
models in which every leading
company releases a new model

6
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:24,650
in every few months.

7
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:29,630
So at the center of this
arms race is math and coding.

8
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:33,110
For example, when
OpenAI introduces o1,

9
00:00:33,110 --> 00:00:35,720
you typically see
this kind of charts

10
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:39,950
showing that its capability
in various benchmarks.

11
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:44,330
And you see the math
competition like AIME

12
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:46,520
and the coding competition
like Codeforces

13
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:48,590
are usually highlighted.

14
00:00:48,590 --> 00:00:51,650
And similarly, when
Google released Gemini

15
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:55,610
and when xAI released
Grok, they also

16
00:00:55,610 --> 00:01:00,050
highlight the capability
in math and coding.

17
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:03,350
And in November '19--

18
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:10,140
in November 2023, XTX Markets,
which is a British hedge fund,

19
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,390
launched the AI/MO prize.

20
00:01:12,390 --> 00:01:17,880
That's $10 million for AI that
can win a gold medal in IMO.

21
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:21,830
And not long after
that, in July 2024,

22
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:26,390
Google DeepMind announced that
AlphaProof and AlphaGeometry

23
00:01:26,390 --> 00:01:29,240
have achieved the
silver medal level.

24
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:33,020
I guess that's the content
of the last lecture.

25
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:37,320
And if you want to go beyond
high school competition math,

26
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:39,950
we have this Epoch AI.

27
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:44,990
It introduced a benchmark
called FrontierMath,

28
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:48,470
which includes research level
mathematical questions that's

29
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:52,490
curated by professional
mathematicians.

30
00:01:52,490 --> 00:01:56,060
And not long after
that, OpenAI o3

31
00:01:56,060 --> 00:02:00,160
announced that it can solve
more than 20% of that.

32
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:04,020
And which came as a surprise
because when FrontierMath

33
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:06,400
benchmark was released,
people thought,

34
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,610
OK, this is going to be--

35
00:02:08,610 --> 00:02:11,460
it's going to take a few
years for language models

36
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:12,940
to make real progress.

37
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:15,400
But really, after
less than one year,

38
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:19,530
we have groundbreaking
results from OpenAI.

39
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:23,970
So why are people interested
in math and coding?

40
00:02:23,970 --> 00:02:26,730
Why are they often highlighted?

41
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:29,700
One reason is that
math and coding

42
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:33,390
are proxies for complex
reasoning and planning.

43
00:02:33,390 --> 00:02:36,090
And reasoning and
planning they are not

44
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:38,110
only important for
human intelligence,

45
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:41,010
but they are also one
of the major challenges

46
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:43,890
for current large
language models.

47
00:02:43,890 --> 00:02:47,320
And they can really unlock
unlimited applications

48
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:50,820
if you can make language
models be able to reason

49
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:52,060
and be able to plan.

50
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:55,590
For example, you can have them
do travel planning for you

51
00:02:55,590 --> 00:03:01,260
and also have language
model agent scheduling,

52
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:03,990
calendar invites for you.

53
00:03:03,990 --> 00:03:05,670
And the second
reason that people

54
00:03:05,670 --> 00:03:07,470
are interested in
math and coding

55
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:10,140
is they are relatively
easy to evaluate.

56
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:12,660
Because for a math
problem, you can

57
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:14,890
check if the answer is correct.

58
00:03:14,890 --> 00:03:17,310
And for coding, you
can run the unit test

59
00:03:17,310 --> 00:03:20,010
to see if they can
pass all the tests.

60
00:03:20,010 --> 00:03:24,220
But if you ask your language
model to write a crime fiction,

61
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:27,760
or you ask it to
compose a symphony,

62
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:31,290
it's going to be much,
much harder to evaluate.

63
00:03:31,290 --> 00:03:35,700
You can argue that
crime fiction also

64
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:39,660
involves reasoning and
composing a symphony definitely

65
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:40,600
need planning.

66
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:42,310
So they also need
reasoning and planning,

67
00:03:42,310 --> 00:03:47,610
but they are just not
as easy to evaluate.

68
00:03:47,610 --> 00:03:53,440
So I put it relatively easy here
because as we will come back,

69
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:56,100
we don't think
checking the answers

70
00:03:56,100 --> 00:03:58,970
or running the unit test
is a perfect evaluation.

71
00:03:58,970 --> 00:04:01,910
We are just arguing that
it's a reasonable evaluation,

72
00:04:01,910 --> 00:04:04,570
and we will revisit
this point later

73
00:04:04,570 --> 00:04:08,560
in the rest of this lecture.

74
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:10,840
OK, so we know
language models are

75
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:14,560
racing to be good
at math and coding,

76
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:18,399
and next we want to briefly
introduce the main techniques

77
00:04:18,399 --> 00:04:19,360
behind them.

78
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:23,650
How do we train language
models to solve math problems?

79
00:04:23,650 --> 00:04:26,590
Here we focus on math,
but the discussion largely

80
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:29,350
also applies to coding as well.

81
00:04:29,350 --> 00:04:34,570
And we will see later that math
and coding are deeply connected.

82
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:37,210
And state of the art
math language models

83
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:40,150
are trained using
mainly two techniques.

84
00:04:40,150 --> 00:04:43,030
One is supervised
fine tuning or SFT,

85
00:04:43,030 --> 00:04:46,520
and the other is
reinforcement learning or RL.

86
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:49,180
And we're going to present
a very high level view

87
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:55,990
and emphasizing the need for
data and for verifiability.

88
00:04:55,990 --> 00:04:57,430
Starting with SFT.

89
00:04:57,430 --> 00:05:01,540
So in SFT, you have
a base model that's

90
00:05:01,540 --> 00:05:05,830
a generic language model
pre-trained on internet scale

91
00:05:05,830 --> 00:05:07,480
data sets.

92
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:10,990
And to learn mathematics,
you give it additional data

93
00:05:10,990 --> 00:05:13,520
that's focused on mathematics.

94
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:17,030
So you can grab web
pages from MathOverflow

95
00:05:17,030 --> 00:05:21,370
or you can have
papers from arXiv.

96
00:05:21,370 --> 00:05:23,770
And the result of
this fine tuning

97
00:05:23,770 --> 00:05:27,370
is called the base
math language model.

98
00:05:27,370 --> 00:05:29,600
Well, it has seen a
lot of mathematics,

99
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:34,250
but it still hasn't yet
learned how to solve problems.

100
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:38,180
So the next step is to
give it a lot of problems

101
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:41,410
and with this detailed
step-by-step solutions

102
00:05:41,410 --> 00:05:44,110
for these problems.

103
00:05:44,110 --> 00:05:49,910
And optionally, in the solution,
you can use external tools.

104
00:05:49,910 --> 00:05:52,900
So here the model is
trained to produce

105
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:54,780
not only natural
language reasoning,

106
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:59,280
but also it can call
external tools like Python.

107
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:02,860
So this is an example of
a problem it can solve.

108
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:10,250
So the most important thing in
this pipeline, as you can see,

109
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:12,890
is data set.

110
00:06:12,890 --> 00:06:16,050
You need data set for
continuous pre-training.

111
00:06:16,050 --> 00:06:20,330
That's the web documents
collected from web

112
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:23,160
from the mass forums
like Stack Overflow.

113
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:28,040
And you also have to annotate
a lot of data for further fine

114
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:32,720
tuning, consisting of problems
with step-by-step solutions.

115
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:38,120
And I would say collecting this
data set is one of the most

116
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:42,560
expensive part in this pipeline,
because it cannot be fully

117
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:43,410
automated.

118
00:06:43,410 --> 00:06:47,460
You need human to annotate
the data, to clean the data,

119
00:06:47,460 --> 00:06:54,260
and to transform the data into
the data set that you can use.

120
00:06:54,260 --> 00:07:00,380
So what if our data set has
only the final answer, but not

121
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:03,050
the intermediate steps?

122
00:07:03,050 --> 00:07:07,880
This is a scenario where you
don't want humans to annotate

123
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:11,090
data very expensively,
but maybe you

124
00:07:11,090 --> 00:07:16,010
can more cheaply get the data
that has only the final answer.

125
00:07:16,010 --> 00:07:19,850
So can we still use this
data set to train the model?

126
00:07:19,850 --> 00:07:21,180
And the answer is yes.

127
00:07:21,180 --> 00:07:22,700
We can use
reinforcement learning

128
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:26,510
to train the model
to generate solutions

129
00:07:26,510 --> 00:07:29,720
with only the problem
and the final answer.

130
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:30,840
Here's how it works.

131
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:35,010
So you have the problem and you
give it to the language model.

132
00:07:35,010 --> 00:07:37,700
And the model
generates a solution

133
00:07:37,700 --> 00:07:40,160
that has intermediate
steps and that

134
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:42,530
also has the final solution.

135
00:07:42,530 --> 00:07:47,300
And you can check if the final
solution is correct by comparing

136
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:48,630
it with the ground truth.

137
00:07:48,630 --> 00:07:52,800
So here, it's a
correct solution.

138
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:55,410
And the model can also
generate incorrect solution

139
00:07:55,410 --> 00:07:57,420
and you can check.

140
00:07:57,420 --> 00:08:00,750
So the result of
this verification

141
00:08:00,750 --> 00:08:04,300
can be used as reward signal
in reinforcement learning,

142
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:08,100
and you can use
algorithms like GRPO

143
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:11,880
to optimize the model
to achieve high rewards.

144
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:16,690
Well, you can treat the reward
for a correct solution to be 1.

145
00:08:16,690 --> 00:08:18,370
And if it's an
incorrect solution,

146
00:08:18,370 --> 00:08:19,840
the reward is going to be 0.

147
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:24,870
So this approach is very simple
approach and very powerful idea.

148
00:08:24,870 --> 00:08:28,620
It's actually the idea is
there for several decades,

149
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:33,659
but it was first shown to work
for training reasoning language

150
00:08:33,659 --> 00:08:35,679
models by DeepSeek-R1.

151
00:08:35,679 --> 00:08:37,350
That's fairly recently.

152
00:08:37,350 --> 00:08:40,919
And this idea is getting
a lot of momentum.

153
00:08:40,919 --> 00:08:42,840
Just to see how much
momentum it has.

154
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:47,740
Just one month ago, two pioneers
in reinforcement learning,

155
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:54,570
Richard Sutton and Andrew
Barto, won the ACM Turing Award.

156
00:08:54,570 --> 00:08:57,030
So here, in
reinforcement learning,

157
00:08:57,030 --> 00:09:00,760
the key is really
the verifiability.

158
00:09:00,760 --> 00:09:03,760
So you have to be able
to verify the results.

159
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:07,630
You have to be able to tell if
the solution is correct or not.

160
00:09:07,630 --> 00:09:10,140
Otherwise, you don't
have the reward.

161
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:14,320
So if the solution has a
final answer, that's a number.

162
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:16,680
If it has a numerical
solution, you

163
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:19,180
can always check by comparing
with the ground truth.

164
00:09:19,180 --> 00:09:21,850
But what if it's a
theorem proving problem?

165
00:09:21,850 --> 00:09:24,100
So you are asked to
generate a proof.

166
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:26,370
But there's really no--

167
00:09:26,370 --> 00:09:31,390
well, no easy way to tell if
the proof is correct or not.

168
00:09:31,390 --> 00:09:33,480
Can we still do
reinforcement learning?

169
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:38,190
We will revisit this point in
the later part of this talk.

170
00:09:38,190 --> 00:09:41,190
So here we have covered
the main techniques

171
00:09:41,190 --> 00:09:45,210
to train language models for
math, supervised learning

172
00:09:45,210 --> 00:09:46,890
and reinforcement learning.

173
00:09:46,890 --> 00:09:52,990
I would say any state-of-the-art
masked language models is kind

174
00:09:52,990 --> 00:09:57,730
of like it's a combination
of a very strong base model.

175
00:09:57,730 --> 00:10:00,970
And these two techniques
for post-training

176
00:10:00,970 --> 00:10:05,440
and maybe more importantly,
marvelous engineering efforts.

177
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:08,270
So the algorithms are
very straightforward.

178
00:10:08,270 --> 00:10:11,290
Pretty much every
leading industry lab

179
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:13,220
is using the same algorithm.

180
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:15,640
But what really
matters is who has

181
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:18,290
the better infrastructure,
better engineering,

182
00:10:18,290 --> 00:10:21,610
and better data set.

183
00:10:21,610 --> 00:10:26,530
So then we will ask, will
AI soon solve mathematics

184
00:10:26,530 --> 00:10:30,250
since we have this
very powerful recipe?

185
00:10:30,250 --> 00:10:32,090
And the answer is definitely no.

186
00:10:32,090 --> 00:10:34,250
So we have a few gaps.

187
00:10:34,250 --> 00:10:38,560
So the first gap is how do
we go from pre-college math

188
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:41,320
to advanced math?

189
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:45,160
Existing successes are
as we see they are mostly

190
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:50,860
limited on pre-college
math like IMO or AIME.

191
00:10:50,860 --> 00:10:53,350
And if you give language
models more advanced

192
00:10:53,350 --> 00:10:55,370
mathematical problems,
they still struggle.

193
00:10:55,370 --> 00:10:58,000
So this quote is
from Terry Tao, who

194
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,570
is one of the most prominent
mathematicians nowadays.

195
00:11:02,570 --> 00:11:06,710
And he said, he
played with OpenAI o1.

196
00:11:06,710 --> 00:11:11,080
And he finds they still struggle
with the most advanced research

197
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:13,240
mathematical tasks.

198
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:18,220
Well, this is kind of expected
because mathematical research

199
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:20,300
is by definition novel.

200
00:11:20,300 --> 00:11:23,530
And since it's novel, you
don't have a lot of data

201
00:11:23,530 --> 00:11:25,780
to train language models.

202
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:32,660
And also, o3 results
on FrontierMath,

203
00:11:32,660 --> 00:11:34,720
as we mentioned before.

204
00:11:34,720 --> 00:11:37,280
They are on advanced
mathematical problems,

205
00:11:37,280 --> 00:11:39,820
but it comes with a few caveats.

206
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:42,910
Well, the first
caveat is that there

207
00:11:42,910 --> 00:11:46,210
are financial ties between
Epoch AI and OpenAI

208
00:11:46,210 --> 00:11:49,680
that were later disclosed.

209
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:52,490
But I would say
more importantly,

210
00:11:52,490 --> 00:11:55,250
there's a difference
between FrontierMath

211
00:11:55,250 --> 00:11:57,450
and real mathematical research.

212
00:11:57,450 --> 00:12:01,760
The problems in
FrontierMath are designed

213
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:04,650
to have numerical solutions,
so they are advanced.

214
00:12:04,650 --> 00:12:07,190
They are very advanced
math, but they are designed

215
00:12:07,190 --> 00:12:08,730
to have a numerical solution.

216
00:12:08,730 --> 00:12:10,550
So this is kind of
counterintuitive

217
00:12:10,550 --> 00:12:15,180
because we know most
problems in advanced math

218
00:12:15,180 --> 00:12:17,630
they don't have
numerical solutions,

219
00:12:17,630 --> 00:12:21,440
but they designed the
benchmark in this way in order

220
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:26,240
for the models to be evaluated.

221
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:30,020
And so this is related
to the second gap.

222
00:12:30,020 --> 00:12:34,250
Current models are very good at
guessing the answer, but how do

223
00:12:34,250 --> 00:12:37,040
we get it to write proofs?

224
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:40,670
And the recent study has
found language models still

225
00:12:40,670 --> 00:12:44,420
struggle in writing
even very basic proofs.

226
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:49,010
So PutnamBench-- Putnam is
the mathematical competition

227
00:12:49,010 --> 00:12:50,960
for undergrads.

228
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:55,760
And people recently tried
using OpenAI's reasoning model

229
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:58,910
on Putnam, and
here's the result.

230
00:12:58,910 --> 00:13:04,730
I'm very unimpressed by ChatGPT
in the recent Putnam exam,

231
00:13:04,730 --> 00:13:06,000
and it can--

232
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,130
most answers are worth one or
two points out of 10 points.

233
00:13:10,130 --> 00:13:14,280
So this is checked by
human mathematician.

234
00:13:14,280 --> 00:13:18,590
And recently, in another
paper, researchers

235
00:13:18,590 --> 00:13:25,500
evaluated language models
proofs for the USAMO exam.

236
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:30,600
And they showed that all
the models scored around 5%,

237
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:33,020
so which is pretty low.

238
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:38,060
And the errors those language
models are making they

239
00:13:38,060 --> 00:13:42,350
can be pretty easy to spot
once you pointed them out.

240
00:13:42,350 --> 00:13:45,600
But if it's in a long proof,
it's very hard to detect them.

241
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:47,370
So here's an example.

242
00:13:47,370 --> 00:13:52,500
We are asking the model to
prove a very simple inequality.

243
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:55,050
So if ABC satisfies
some condition

244
00:13:55,050 --> 00:13:58,180
and then there should be
some-- you have some results.

245
00:13:58,180 --> 00:14:00,480
And here it's making an error.

246
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:05,040
When it tries to multiply
an existing inequality

247
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:09,750
by a positive number, it
should preserve the direction

248
00:14:09,750 --> 00:14:11,110
of the inequality.

249
00:14:11,110 --> 00:14:15,450
But it flipped the direction,
so that's a very simple error

250
00:14:15,450 --> 00:14:17,120
if you point it out.

251
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:25,330
So we argue that
current language models,

252
00:14:25,330 --> 00:14:28,710
the current approach for
training language models

253
00:14:28,710 --> 00:14:32,640
is not sufficient for
solving these problems

254
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:38,500
because it relies heavily on
data and on verifiability.

255
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:41,710
And when you work with advanced
mathematics, as we said,

256
00:14:41,710 --> 00:14:43,060
you don't have enough data.

257
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:45,750
The data is very scarce.

258
00:14:45,750 --> 00:14:49,740
And when you work with proofs,
you don't have verifiability.

259
00:14:49,740 --> 00:14:57,420
And in advanced mathematics,
most problems requires proofs.

260
00:14:57,420 --> 00:15:02,790
So if language models are not
enough, what else do we need?

261
00:15:02,790 --> 00:15:06,270
In a recent position paper
we released on arXiv,

262
00:15:06,270 --> 00:15:08,880
we argued that one
important missing

263
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:12,510
point is formal reasoning.

264
00:15:12,510 --> 00:15:17,160
Here, formal reasoning means
you grant mathematical reasoning

265
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:18,570
in formal systems.

266
00:15:18,570 --> 00:15:21,780
And formal systems they
can include first order

267
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:24,330
logic, higher order
logic, dependent type

268
00:15:24,330 --> 00:15:28,770
theory, or even computer
programs and their semantics.

269
00:15:28,770 --> 00:15:30,900
And the role of
formal system here

270
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:35,280
is to try to verify the proofs
and provide automatic feedback

271
00:15:35,280 --> 00:15:37,020
on the proofs.

272
00:15:37,020 --> 00:15:40,650
So this is very useful because
the feedback it provides

273
00:15:40,650 --> 00:15:43,300
can mitigate data scarcity.

274
00:15:43,300 --> 00:15:46,460
You can have the model learn
from automatic feedback,

275
00:15:46,460 --> 00:15:51,080
and you don't need to collect
as much human created data.

276
00:15:51,080 --> 00:15:54,260
And second, now you
can do verification,

277
00:15:54,260 --> 00:15:59,590
which enable you to evaluate
the model rigorously.

278
00:15:59,590 --> 00:16:03,100
So therefore, we argue
that to move forward we

279
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:05,590
really need to integrate
formal reasoning

280
00:16:05,590 --> 00:16:09,250
into the current approach.

281
00:16:09,250 --> 00:16:12,280
So we're going to
very briefly introduce

282
00:16:12,280 --> 00:16:19,030
Lean, which is a kind of formal
system we focus on in this talk.

283
00:16:19,030 --> 00:16:21,800
And it's also used
in AlphaProof,

284
00:16:21,800 --> 00:16:25,300
so I guess people already
have some familiarity on Lean.

285
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:28,760
So here is here's
a programming Lean.

286
00:16:28,760 --> 00:16:32,270
In this program you first
define natural numbers.

287
00:16:32,270 --> 00:16:35,470
So a natural number is
either 0 or a successor

288
00:16:35,470 --> 00:16:36,920
of another natural number.

289
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:39,500
And then you define addition
between two natural numbers,

290
00:16:39,500 --> 00:16:41,650
so this is just a
recursive function.

291
00:16:41,650 --> 00:16:44,620
And then you can state
and prove some theorems

292
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:46,520
about natural
numbers and addition.

293
00:16:46,520 --> 00:16:52,240
For example, you can prove
a plus b equals 2b plus a.

294
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:56,330
And this here is only
one file in Lean.

295
00:16:56,330 --> 00:16:59,900
And if you are using Lean
to write formal mathematics,

296
00:16:59,900 --> 00:17:04,210
it's similar to using Python
or another programming language

297
00:17:04,210 --> 00:17:05,690
to develop software.

298
00:17:05,690 --> 00:17:10,599
So you have one file and
this file has the proof,

299
00:17:10,599 --> 00:17:13,849
and the proof is
it has semantics.

300
00:17:13,849 --> 00:17:18,280
So you are starting-- it
corresponds to a proof tree.

301
00:17:18,280 --> 00:17:21,430
At the root of this tree
is the original theorem

302
00:17:21,430 --> 00:17:23,349
you want to prove.

303
00:17:23,349 --> 00:17:27,760
And every step in this proof
decomposes the proof goal

304
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:31,120
into several simpler
subgoals, and you

305
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:35,140
can do that recursively until
all the subgoals are solved.

306
00:17:35,140 --> 00:17:39,430
And this is one file, and files
are organized into larger units

307
00:17:39,430 --> 00:17:43,760
like a project can
have many files.

308
00:17:43,760 --> 00:17:46,770
And the project can be
open sourced on GitHub.

309
00:17:46,770 --> 00:17:51,120
For example, in your project,
you can define real numbers.

310
00:17:51,120 --> 00:17:52,880
And someone else's
project can build

311
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:54,660
on your definitions
of real numbers,

312
00:17:54,660 --> 00:17:59,990
and they can formalize, for
example, optimization theory.

313
00:17:59,990 --> 00:18:03,530
So this is similar to
how you develop software.

314
00:18:03,530 --> 00:18:09,360
And one example of using
AI with Lean is AlphaProof.

315
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:12,180
That's the content
of the last lecture,

316
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:15,780
and it's a very
successful example.

317
00:18:15,780 --> 00:18:20,480
And it uses Lean to
generate a lot of theorems

318
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:24,590
and a lot of proofs and
verify those proofs,

319
00:18:24,590 --> 00:18:28,430
and do search and reinforcement
learning to learn the model.

320
00:18:28,430 --> 00:18:30,830
So in the rest of
this lecture, we

321
00:18:30,830 --> 00:18:34,430
will be talking about
mainly two tasks

322
00:18:34,430 --> 00:18:38,480
at the center of
using Lean and AI.

323
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:40,640
So first is theorem proving.

324
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:44,900
In theorem proving, your
input is a theorem statement

325
00:18:44,900 --> 00:18:48,510
and you want the model
to generate a proof.

326
00:18:48,510 --> 00:18:50,600
Here, both the
statement and the proof

327
00:18:50,600 --> 00:18:53,750
are written formally
in Lean, and the Lean

328
00:18:53,750 --> 00:18:55,890
can check if the proof
is correct or not.

329
00:18:55,890 --> 00:18:59,480
So there's no room for
the model to hallucinate.

330
00:18:59,480 --> 00:19:02,340
And the other task is
auto formalization.

331
00:19:02,340 --> 00:19:05,550
In autoformalization you have
the informal mathematics,

332
00:19:05,550 --> 00:19:07,700
for example, from
a textbook, and you

333
00:19:07,700 --> 00:19:10,370
want to use your
model to translate it

334
00:19:10,370 --> 00:19:13,250
into formal mathematics.

335
00:19:13,250 --> 00:19:17,870
So next we will be first
focusing on theorem proving.

336
00:19:17,870 --> 00:19:20,960
And a very straightforward
way to do theorem proving

337
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:24,390
is you just give the model
what you want to prove,

338
00:19:24,390 --> 00:19:27,990
and you ask the model to
generate a step in the proof

339
00:19:27,990 --> 00:19:30,720
or you can ask it to
generate the entire proof.

340
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:35,160
And well, because if you
can generate the next step,

341
00:19:35,160 --> 00:19:38,540
you can kind of using
search to chain steps

342
00:19:38,540 --> 00:19:40,450
together into a complete proof.

343
00:19:40,450 --> 00:19:44,040
So I would say,
the central problem

344
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:46,340
here is how do you
generate the next step?

345
00:19:49,140 --> 00:19:51,210
And a very
straightforward solution

346
00:19:51,210 --> 00:19:55,200
is, OK, we have human
written formal proofs.

347
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:59,610
And we can train a model
to do supervised learning

348
00:19:59,610 --> 00:20:01,980
on those data so that
the model can learn

349
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:03,780
how to generate the next step.

350
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:10,110
And this idea has been
actually here for a while.

351
00:20:10,110 --> 00:20:12,780
And earlier work used
classical machine

352
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:16,200
learning algorithms like
k nearest neighborhoods.

353
00:20:16,200 --> 00:20:19,230
And around 2019,
people have started

354
00:20:19,230 --> 00:20:22,030
to use deep neural
networks for this task.

355
00:20:22,030 --> 00:20:24,120
And even later,
people are starting

356
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:26,520
to use large language models.

357
00:20:26,520 --> 00:20:31,840
And I would say until the
use of large language models,

358
00:20:31,840 --> 00:20:34,650
we really see a boost
in the performance

359
00:20:34,650 --> 00:20:38,880
because the model can acquire
a lot of mathematical knowledge

360
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:42,575
during pre-training and use that
knowledge in theorem proving.

361
00:20:46,080 --> 00:20:50,775
So we will be mainly talking
about this paper called

362
00:20:50,775 --> 00:20:55,020
LeanDojo, which was
published in NeurIPS 2023.

363
00:20:55,020 --> 00:21:01,090
So, well, in this paper we
provide open source data sets,

364
00:21:01,090 --> 00:21:04,350
tools, and models for
theorem proving in Lean.

365
00:21:04,350 --> 00:21:06,790
And compared to
previous approaches,

366
00:21:06,790 --> 00:21:10,230
the main difference is first
we are completely open.

367
00:21:10,230 --> 00:21:13,800
Previous approaches
are private and they

368
00:21:13,800 --> 00:21:16,530
don't release the
data set and models

369
00:21:16,530 --> 00:21:20,520
and they were done
in private labs.

370
00:21:20,520 --> 00:21:23,400
And second, we
propose a new model

371
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:27,060
to generate the next
step in the proof.

372
00:21:27,060 --> 00:21:29,490
So here is an overview.

373
00:21:29,490 --> 00:21:35,400
So in Lean, we can extract data
from human written Lean code.

374
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:40,670
We extract about 10,000
theorems and proofs.

375
00:21:40,670 --> 00:21:44,150
And for each proof, we have
the steps in the proof.

376
00:21:44,150 --> 00:21:48,070
And we also have the
lemmas used in the proof

377
00:21:48,070 --> 00:21:50,750
and also the definitions
of these lemmas.

378
00:21:50,750 --> 00:21:52,730
And after extracting
this data set,

379
00:21:52,730 --> 00:21:55,550
we use this data set to train
a machine learning model.

380
00:21:55,550 --> 00:21:57,760
And once the model
is trained, the model

381
00:21:57,760 --> 00:22:01,610
can interact with Lean
to prove new theorem.

382
00:22:01,610 --> 00:22:05,360
And so here is the model we
introduced for this task.

383
00:22:05,360 --> 00:22:09,520
It's called Retrieval-Augmented
Prover or ReProver.

384
00:22:09,520 --> 00:22:12,050
The motivation is that
when you prove a theorem,

385
00:22:12,050 --> 00:22:15,050
you almost never
start from scratch.

386
00:22:15,050 --> 00:22:18,340
You use existing lemmas.

387
00:22:18,340 --> 00:22:20,510
And in order to use
the existing lemmas,

388
00:22:20,510 --> 00:22:23,020
the model have to know
what lemmas are there

389
00:22:23,020 --> 00:22:25,610
and try to decide whether
a lemma is useful.

390
00:22:25,610 --> 00:22:28,300
So here, given a
proof state, that's

391
00:22:28,300 --> 00:22:29,570
the goal you want to prove.

392
00:22:29,570 --> 00:22:34,930
And also given a library of
lemmas, so first do retrieval.

393
00:22:34,930 --> 00:22:38,330
In this phase, we encode
everything into vectors.

394
00:22:38,330 --> 00:22:43,660
So the state is now a vector
and each lemma becomes a vector.

395
00:22:43,660 --> 00:22:46,660
And we compute the
similarity between the state

396
00:22:46,660 --> 00:22:49,840
and each lemma, and
we use the result

397
00:22:49,840 --> 00:22:53,290
to retrieve the top lemmas.

398
00:22:53,290 --> 00:22:55,370
And after the lemmas
are retrieved,

399
00:22:55,370 --> 00:22:57,790
we combine the
lemmas with the state

400
00:22:57,790 --> 00:23:03,940
and use another language model
to generate the next step.

401
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:06,890
And so here, the
model is very simple,

402
00:23:06,890 --> 00:23:11,300
but the really important point
is how do you get the data set?

403
00:23:11,300 --> 00:23:14,860
And as we said in
LeanDojo you can

404
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:18,040
extract the data set from Lean.

405
00:23:18,040 --> 00:23:22,390
From Lean projects like MathLib
and other Lean products.

406
00:23:22,390 --> 00:23:28,180
So here is a summary of a
typical neural theorem prover.

407
00:23:28,180 --> 00:23:30,110
So on the left, you see a tree.

408
00:23:30,110 --> 00:23:33,340
So that's the tree
during proof search.

409
00:23:33,340 --> 00:23:35,680
You have an initial
state and your model

410
00:23:35,680 --> 00:23:38,880
can generate multiple
potential next steps.

411
00:23:38,880 --> 00:23:42,140
And you can run those steps,
which gives you the next proof

412
00:23:42,140 --> 00:23:42,810
state.

413
00:23:42,810 --> 00:23:46,130
And you can do that repeatedly
until you find a proof.

414
00:23:46,130 --> 00:23:48,380
So for this part, you can
use any search algorithm

415
00:23:48,380 --> 00:23:51,950
like DFS or Monte
Carlo tree search.

416
00:23:51,950 --> 00:23:57,180
And the second part is, how
do you generate the next step?

417
00:23:57,180 --> 00:24:02,180
So on the right, you see at each
node in this proof search tree,

418
00:24:02,180 --> 00:24:04,410
you have the current state.

419
00:24:04,410 --> 00:24:06,980
So you give the current
state to the language model

420
00:24:06,980 --> 00:24:09,990
and it generates
suggestions for you.

421
00:24:09,990 --> 00:24:12,200
And here optionally,
the language model

422
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:17,300
can take a formal math library
which is the library of lemmas

423
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:17,890
you can use.

424
00:24:21,140 --> 00:24:24,110
And so after the
LeanDojo work, we've

425
00:24:24,110 --> 00:24:28,020
seen a bunch of work
trying to improve on that.

426
00:24:28,020 --> 00:24:29,810
For example, one
thing you could do

427
00:24:29,810 --> 00:24:32,970
is what we call
expert iteration.

428
00:24:32,970 --> 00:24:37,530
So which is so
you have a prover.

429
00:24:37,530 --> 00:24:39,110
Even if it's not
very good, you can

430
00:24:39,110 --> 00:24:42,980
use it to attempt to
prove some new theorems.

431
00:24:42,980 --> 00:24:46,050
And in this process, you
will get some new proofs.

432
00:24:46,050 --> 00:24:47,750
And once you get
the new proofs, you

433
00:24:47,750 --> 00:24:49,590
can add them into
your training set.

434
00:24:49,590 --> 00:24:51,240
So now you have a
better training set,

435
00:24:51,240 --> 00:24:52,698
and you can use
the better training

436
00:24:52,698 --> 00:24:54,390
set to train a better prover.

437
00:24:54,390 --> 00:24:56,270
And you can iterate.

438
00:24:56,270 --> 00:25:02,010
So in this way, we show that
if you run for more iterations,

439
00:25:02,010 --> 00:25:06,110
the performance usually improves
until you hit some saturation

440
00:25:06,110 --> 00:25:07,640
point.

441
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:12,020
So we've seen a very
simple way to build

442
00:25:12,020 --> 00:25:13,830
a theorem-proving model.

443
00:25:13,830 --> 00:25:16,290
You just extract
human written proofs,

444
00:25:16,290 --> 00:25:19,850
and you train a language model
to generate the next step

445
00:25:19,850 --> 00:25:21,440
in the proof.

446
00:25:21,440 --> 00:25:26,000
But we would say
it's a great step

447
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:27,870
but it comes with limitations.

448
00:25:27,870 --> 00:25:32,180
So the first limitation
is still data.

449
00:25:32,180 --> 00:25:34,390
Because language
models work well only

450
00:25:34,390 --> 00:25:37,070
if you have a lot
of training data,

451
00:25:37,070 --> 00:25:40,570
and the amount of human
written proofs on the internet

452
00:25:40,570 --> 00:25:45,130
is very limited compared
to say Python code.

453
00:25:45,130 --> 00:25:49,210
And also another limitation
is inherent to the task

454
00:25:49,210 --> 00:25:50,240
of theorem proving.

455
00:25:50,240 --> 00:25:53,710
So if you think
about other tasks

456
00:25:53,710 --> 00:25:56,570
that AI has been
successful like playing Go.

457
00:25:56,570 --> 00:25:58,840
So what's the difference
between playing Go

458
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:01,780
and proving a theorem?

459
00:26:01,780 --> 00:26:05,210
The main difference is that in
Go you have a 19 by 19 board,

460
00:26:05,210 --> 00:26:07,780
so the possible
action you can take

461
00:26:07,780 --> 00:26:09,980
is constrained by the
size of the board.

462
00:26:09,980 --> 00:26:10,910
It's finite.

463
00:26:10,910 --> 00:26:14,230
But in mathematics,
arguably you can--

464
00:26:14,230 --> 00:26:16,630
your action space is
infinite, so there

465
00:26:16,630 --> 00:26:18,880
is infinite number of
things you could potentially

466
00:26:18,880 --> 00:26:21,400
do when proving a theorem.

467
00:26:21,400 --> 00:26:23,720
So since the action
space is infinite,

468
00:26:23,720 --> 00:26:27,490
it's really hard to
cover the entire space

469
00:26:27,490 --> 00:26:31,370
if you only learn from
human created data sets.

470
00:26:31,370 --> 00:26:34,150
And even if you use
reinforcement learning,

471
00:26:34,150 --> 00:26:37,030
it's very hard to explore
the space efficiently

472
00:26:37,030 --> 00:26:39,202
during reinforcement learning.

473
00:26:39,202 --> 00:26:40,160
That's a good question.

474
00:26:40,160 --> 00:26:44,570
So in the work we
have talked up to now,

475
00:26:44,570 --> 00:26:47,260
we are just using
supervised learning.

476
00:26:47,260 --> 00:26:49,970
So there's no
curriculum involved.

477
00:26:49,970 --> 00:26:51,610
But if you use
reinforcement learning,

478
00:26:51,610 --> 00:26:53,800
it's natural to
have the model first

479
00:26:53,800 --> 00:26:56,740
learn from relatively
simple examples

480
00:26:56,740 --> 00:26:58,870
and then try to generalize.

481
00:26:58,870 --> 00:27:02,860
And if you look at more recent
work in using reinforcement

482
00:27:02,860 --> 00:27:05,830
learning for theorem
proving, one major difficulty

483
00:27:05,830 --> 00:27:11,630
is how do you choose examples
to present to the model?

484
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:15,235
So you want the examples to be
not too easy, but also not too

485
00:27:15,235 --> 00:27:15,735
hard.

486
00:27:19,630 --> 00:27:24,940
So how do we solve the
limitation regarding the action

487
00:27:24,940 --> 00:27:26,170
space?

488
00:27:26,170 --> 00:27:28,030
So in a more recent
work we tried

489
00:27:28,030 --> 00:27:30,670
to take a step in
that direction,

490
00:27:30,670 --> 00:27:36,740
but we choose a very limited
domain as a playground.

491
00:27:36,740 --> 00:27:40,870
So we prove inequalities
in Math Olympiad.

492
00:27:40,870 --> 00:27:43,090
So inequalities in
Math Olympiad is

493
00:27:43,090 --> 00:27:46,870
like you have a few
real numbers like ABC,

494
00:27:46,870 --> 00:27:49,310
and they satisfy
some precondition.

495
00:27:49,310 --> 00:27:52,570
And you want to prove a
postcondition like this.

496
00:27:52,570 --> 00:27:55,510
And we choose this
domain because the proofs

497
00:27:55,510 --> 00:27:58,270
in this domain is
highly regular.

498
00:27:58,270 --> 00:28:00,610
So here is what humans would do.

499
00:28:00,610 --> 00:28:03,260
Humans would do
typically two things.

500
00:28:03,260 --> 00:28:08,890
One is you can apply an existing
lemma like AM-GM inequality

501
00:28:08,890 --> 00:28:10,940
or Cauchy-Schwarz inequality.

502
00:28:10,940 --> 00:28:12,670
And the other thing
people typically

503
00:28:12,670 --> 00:28:17,440
do is to rewrite the
current formula into a form

504
00:28:17,440 --> 00:28:21,820
so that an existing
lemma becomes applicable.

505
00:28:21,820 --> 00:28:23,840
But even with these
two kind of actions,

506
00:28:23,840 --> 00:28:25,930
the action space
is still very big.

507
00:28:25,930 --> 00:28:30,010
For example, for proving
this particular inequality,

508
00:28:30,010 --> 00:28:32,380
you can apply AM-GM.

509
00:28:32,380 --> 00:28:35,120
And AM-GM can be applied
in more than one way,

510
00:28:35,120 --> 00:28:38,960
so you have very big
branching factor.

511
00:28:38,960 --> 00:28:42,070
And you can also
apply other inequality

512
00:28:42,070 --> 00:28:45,070
like the T2 inequality,
and you can also

513
00:28:45,070 --> 00:28:49,220
potentially choose to rewrite
the formula into this form.

514
00:28:49,220 --> 00:28:54,130
So here the actions you can
take is essentially infinite.

515
00:28:54,130 --> 00:28:59,740
So how do we navigate this
kind of infinite action space?

516
00:28:59,740 --> 00:29:04,300
So one thing we do before trying
to solve the problem is, OK,

517
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:07,400
let's try to see how o1
can solve this problem.

518
00:29:07,400 --> 00:29:10,550
And we give a lot of
inequality problems to o1

519
00:29:10,550 --> 00:29:13,240
and we manually
check their proofs.

520
00:29:13,240 --> 00:29:19,030
So as a result, we find a lot
of errors in the o1 produced

521
00:29:19,030 --> 00:29:20,290
proofs.

522
00:29:20,290 --> 00:29:24,760
And we checked 20 examples.

523
00:29:24,760 --> 00:29:28,780
o1 preview proved none
of those examples.

524
00:29:28,780 --> 00:29:33,650
And the more powerful o3
and also the DeepSeek-R1,

525
00:29:33,650 --> 00:29:37,460
also only proved 3 or
4 out of 20 examples.

526
00:29:37,460 --> 00:29:41,400
Whereas a typical gold medalist,
like a human gold medalist,

527
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:43,320
can prove at least 15.

528
00:29:43,320 --> 00:29:46,260
So then let's come
to our method.

529
00:29:46,260 --> 00:29:50,210
So as we said, we
categorize the steps

530
00:29:50,210 --> 00:29:54,830
you can take in this particular
problem into two kinds.

531
00:29:54,830 --> 00:29:57,920
First, we call it scaling.

532
00:29:57,920 --> 00:30:02,600
Scaling is you apply
an existing lemma

533
00:30:02,600 --> 00:30:06,930
like Cauchy-Schwarz to a
part of the current formula.

534
00:30:06,930 --> 00:30:11,000
So in this example, you
find that the left part

535
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:16,460
of the formula can be
used for Cauchy-Schwarz

536
00:30:16,460 --> 00:30:22,460
and which gives you the
result on the right hand side.

537
00:30:22,460 --> 00:30:26,900
So what's special about scaling
is the number of scaling

538
00:30:26,900 --> 00:30:28,980
you can do is finite.

539
00:30:28,980 --> 00:30:32,610
This is because you only have
a finite number of lemmas,

540
00:30:32,610 --> 00:30:36,420
and each lemma can be applied
in only a finite number of ways.

541
00:30:36,420 --> 00:30:41,150
So it's actually
possible to enumerate

542
00:30:41,150 --> 00:30:44,240
all the possible
scalings in this space.

543
00:30:44,240 --> 00:30:49,220
And besides scaling, another
category is called rewriting.

544
00:30:49,220 --> 00:30:51,650
Rewriting is you
are given a formula

545
00:30:51,650 --> 00:30:54,500
and you want to transform
the current formula

546
00:30:54,500 --> 00:30:56,150
into another form.

547
00:30:56,150 --> 00:30:59,150
And rewriting is very different
from scaling in the sense

548
00:30:59,150 --> 00:31:00,660
that the space is infinite.

549
00:31:00,660 --> 00:31:02,690
So given any formula,
there are infinite number

550
00:31:02,690 --> 00:31:06,230
of ways you can rewrite it.

551
00:31:06,230 --> 00:31:09,110
So the key insight
here is that after we

552
00:31:09,110 --> 00:31:11,750
make this categorization,
our algorithm

553
00:31:11,750 --> 00:31:14,750
can enumerate all
the possible scalings

554
00:31:14,750 --> 00:31:16,770
using a symbolic algorithm.

555
00:31:16,770 --> 00:31:20,720
Whereas the rewriting,
selects the infinite space,

556
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:23,600
can be handled by
large language models.

557
00:31:23,600 --> 00:31:28,560
So we combine the
next step generated

558
00:31:28,560 --> 00:31:31,510
by the symbolic algorithm
with language models,

559
00:31:31,510 --> 00:31:34,140
and we use some
heuristics to filter

560
00:31:34,140 --> 00:31:36,760
the results to reduce
the number of goals

561
00:31:36,760 --> 00:31:38,910
you have to improve search.

562
00:31:38,910 --> 00:31:45,120
And as a result, we solved
a much more problems

563
00:31:45,120 --> 00:31:49,600
compared to DeepSeek-R1
and also o3.

564
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:53,470
So here on the top you
see R1 proved 4 theorems.

565
00:31:53,470 --> 00:31:57,510
Humans can prove 15,
whereas our system called

566
00:31:57,510 --> 00:32:00,150
LIPS can prove 16 theorems.

567
00:32:00,150 --> 00:32:06,090
And at the bottom, we evaluate
our system with a few benchmarks

568
00:32:06,090 --> 00:32:08,400
and compare with
existing methods.

569
00:32:08,400 --> 00:32:11,190
So here's one example,
one interesting example,

570
00:32:11,190 --> 00:32:14,430
proof discovered by our system.

571
00:32:14,430 --> 00:32:16,740
So here is interesting
because it's

572
00:32:16,740 --> 00:32:21,330
from Evan Chen, who is IMO
coach for the Team USA.

573
00:32:21,330 --> 00:32:23,680
And he has a problem set.

574
00:32:23,680 --> 00:32:29,770
And he said, this problem has
no hope to be solved by AM-GM.

575
00:32:29,770 --> 00:32:33,030
But we find that the proof
discovered by our system

576
00:32:33,030 --> 00:32:37,830
uses only AM-GM combined
with clever rewriting.

577
00:32:37,830 --> 00:32:41,010
So it can actually find
proofs that humans do not

578
00:32:41,010 --> 00:32:43,500
know how to find.

579
00:32:43,500 --> 00:32:45,900
So here is the takeaway.

580
00:32:45,900 --> 00:32:49,830
The challenge in theorem proving
is how do you efficiently

581
00:32:49,830 --> 00:32:53,370
explore a potentially
infinite action space?

582
00:32:53,370 --> 00:32:55,630
And while this is a
very hard problem,

583
00:32:55,630 --> 00:32:59,560
but if you are working on a
specific domain of mathematics,

584
00:32:59,560 --> 00:33:02,520
you can use domain insight
to structure the action

585
00:33:02,520 --> 00:33:07,620
space so that the action
space becomes more feasible.

586
00:33:07,620 --> 00:33:10,980
But it is an open
problem of whether you

587
00:33:10,980 --> 00:33:14,820
can generalize this approach
to be domain general.

588
00:33:14,820 --> 00:33:17,610
So after talking
about theorem proving,

589
00:33:17,610 --> 00:33:20,370
we will be going to
auto formalization.

590
00:33:20,370 --> 00:33:27,730
And feel free to bring it
up any questions any time.

591
00:33:27,730 --> 00:33:30,850
So in autoformalization,
we have informal math

592
00:33:30,850 --> 00:33:31,880
and we have formal math.

593
00:33:31,880 --> 00:33:35,530
And we want the model to be able
to build a bridge between them,

594
00:33:35,530 --> 00:33:39,550
translating from
informal to formal.

595
00:33:39,550 --> 00:33:44,480
And here, actually, we have two
slightly different subproblems.

596
00:33:44,480 --> 00:33:49,150
First is how do we autoformalize
the theorem statement?

597
00:33:49,150 --> 00:33:53,050
And second is, how do we
autoformalize the proofs?

598
00:33:53,050 --> 00:33:57,310
In statement autoformalization,
the model only takes a statement

599
00:33:57,310 --> 00:34:00,100
and produces the
statement in Lean.

600
00:34:00,100 --> 00:34:02,920
But in proof
autoformalization, the model

601
00:34:02,920 --> 00:34:06,160
have the informal statement,
the informal proof,

602
00:34:06,160 --> 00:34:08,920
and also the formal
statement and it only

603
00:34:08,920 --> 00:34:12,159
produces the formal proof.

604
00:34:12,159 --> 00:34:17,389
So these two subproblems
have different challenges.

605
00:34:17,389 --> 00:34:19,870
Let's talk about statement
formalization first.

606
00:34:19,870 --> 00:34:22,810
For formalizing the
statement, it's really hard

607
00:34:22,810 --> 00:34:25,270
to do the evaluation.

608
00:34:25,270 --> 00:34:29,230
Because, for example, here
the informal statement says

609
00:34:29,230 --> 00:34:31,940
there exists an infinite
number of primes.

610
00:34:31,940 --> 00:34:35,800
And the formal statement says,
for any natural number n,

611
00:34:35,800 --> 00:34:40,179
there exists a p that's
greater than or equal to n.

612
00:34:40,179 --> 00:34:44,380
p is prime, so this is
obviously a correct translation

613
00:34:44,380 --> 00:34:49,090
of the informal statement
because it implies the infinity.

614
00:34:49,090 --> 00:34:52,429
But the problem is it's not
the only incorrect solution.

615
00:34:52,429 --> 00:34:56,650
So if we produce
this solution, we

616
00:34:56,650 --> 00:35:00,640
change p greater than or
equal to n to be strictly

617
00:35:00,640 --> 00:35:03,980
greater than n, while
essentially it's the same.

618
00:35:03,980 --> 00:35:06,440
And we can even make
it more different.

619
00:35:06,440 --> 00:35:09,160
We can make n also a prime.

620
00:35:09,160 --> 00:35:14,090
And for any human you can easily
see, OK, this three statements,

621
00:35:14,090 --> 00:35:15,710
well, they are
slightly different,

622
00:35:15,710 --> 00:35:18,400
but they are essentially
the same thing.

623
00:35:18,400 --> 00:35:20,660
But this creates a problem
for machine learning.

624
00:35:20,660 --> 00:35:23,580
So if the machine learning
model produces one of them.

625
00:35:23,580 --> 00:35:28,160
How do you know if it's
equivalent to the ground truth?

626
00:35:28,160 --> 00:35:33,560
You may want to say, OK, can I
check the logical equivalence?

627
00:35:33,560 --> 00:35:36,740
But the reality is
this kind of checking

628
00:35:36,740 --> 00:35:38,540
the equivalence
between two theorems

629
00:35:38,540 --> 00:35:42,020
is computationally
infeasible, in general.

630
00:35:42,020 --> 00:35:44,690
And in practice,
researchers have

631
00:35:44,690 --> 00:35:48,110
resorted to heuristics
or human evaluation.

632
00:35:48,110 --> 00:35:50,910
But if you rely on
human evaluation,

633
00:35:50,910 --> 00:35:53,610
it's going to be very
expensive and not reproducible.

634
00:35:53,610 --> 00:35:58,260
And if you resort to proxy
metrics or heuristics,

635
00:35:58,260 --> 00:36:00,950
it's not going to be accurate.

636
00:36:00,950 --> 00:36:02,810
So that's the challenge
for evaluating

637
00:36:02,810 --> 00:36:04,650
the autoformalized statement.

638
00:36:04,650 --> 00:36:06,530
How about proofs?

639
00:36:06,530 --> 00:36:09,170
Proofs we have
another challenge.

640
00:36:09,170 --> 00:36:12,690
Human written proofs are even
if they are very rigorous,

641
00:36:12,690 --> 00:36:15,620
they are not rigorous
enough to be formalized.

642
00:36:15,620 --> 00:36:17,540
They often have holes.

643
00:36:17,540 --> 00:36:21,380
One kind of hole is like
mathematicians often

644
00:36:21,380 --> 00:36:23,940
say, OK, this case is
left to the reader.

645
00:36:23,940 --> 00:36:26,870
So if it's left to
the reader, your model

646
00:36:26,870 --> 00:36:29,460
has no way to figure
it out from the text.

647
00:36:29,460 --> 00:36:32,120
It has to figure it
out by their own.

648
00:36:32,120 --> 00:36:34,670
And there are more subtle
holes in the proofs

649
00:36:34,670 --> 00:36:36,210
that we will cover later.

650
00:36:36,210 --> 00:36:39,810
But for the model to be able
to auto formalize the proof,

651
00:36:39,810 --> 00:36:43,440
it kind of has to fill in
all those kind of gaps,

652
00:36:43,440 --> 00:36:46,070
which can be very
difficult. So we've

653
00:36:46,070 --> 00:36:48,590
covered two key challenges
in autoformalization.

654
00:36:48,590 --> 00:36:52,530
For statement, we don't have a
reliable automatic evaluation.

655
00:36:52,530 --> 00:36:56,460
And for proofs, the model has to
fill in all the reasoning gaps.

656
00:36:56,460 --> 00:37:00,720
And in this project, we want
to address both problems.

657
00:37:00,720 --> 00:37:03,300
So that sounds very intractable.

658
00:37:03,300 --> 00:37:04,940
But a lesson we
learned in this project

659
00:37:04,940 --> 00:37:08,030
is that since
intractable in general

660
00:37:08,030 --> 00:37:10,790
can be made tractable
if you restrict

661
00:37:10,790 --> 00:37:13,040
to a very specific domain.

662
00:37:13,040 --> 00:37:16,610
And the domain we choose
is Euclidean geometry.

663
00:37:16,610 --> 00:37:20,960
Euclidean geometry, it's
one of the oldest branches

664
00:37:20,960 --> 00:37:25,170
of mathematics, and it has been
used to test human intelligence

665
00:37:25,170 --> 00:37:27,250
for more than 2000 years.

666
00:37:27,250 --> 00:37:31,560
And recently it has
become like of interest

667
00:37:31,560 --> 00:37:35,580
by the AI community because of
the alpha geometry work, where

668
00:37:35,580 --> 00:37:41,550
they have an AI that can
solve IMO low level geometry.

669
00:37:41,550 --> 00:37:47,130
So in this work, we show that
Euclidean geometry is not only

670
00:37:47,130 --> 00:37:49,810
a good domain for
theorem proving.

671
00:37:49,810 --> 00:37:52,570
It's also a good domain
for formalization.

672
00:37:52,570 --> 00:37:57,810
And more concretely, we collect
a benchmark called LeanEuclid

673
00:37:57,810 --> 00:37:59,440
in Euclidean geometry.

674
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:05,100
It consists of 48 problems
from Euclid's Elements Book I

675
00:38:05,100 --> 00:38:07,720
and more than 100
problems from UniGeo,

676
00:38:07,720 --> 00:38:11,040
which is an existing data set.

677
00:38:11,040 --> 00:38:16,290
So let's look at one
example in our data set.

678
00:38:16,290 --> 00:38:20,070
So this is directly
from Euclid's Elements.

679
00:38:20,070 --> 00:38:24,060
Its proposition one from
book I. It's basically you

680
00:38:24,060 --> 00:38:25,980
have two points,
a and b, and you

681
00:38:25,980 --> 00:38:28,470
want to show that it's
possible to construct

682
00:38:28,470 --> 00:38:30,750
an equilateral triangle.

683
00:38:30,750 --> 00:38:33,780
So for each problem,
you have the diagram

684
00:38:33,780 --> 00:38:37,080
and the informal theorem
statement and also

685
00:38:37,080 --> 00:38:38,880
the informal proof.

686
00:38:38,880 --> 00:38:41,070
And when constructing
the benchmark,

687
00:38:41,070 --> 00:38:45,690
we try to manually formalize
this problem in Lean.

688
00:38:45,690 --> 00:38:50,160
So on the right hand side is
our formalization in Lean.

689
00:38:50,160 --> 00:38:53,010
As you can see, it
corresponds very closely

690
00:38:53,010 --> 00:38:56,100
to the informal version.

691
00:38:56,100 --> 00:39:01,900
The first step is you draw a
circle BCD that's centered at A

692
00:39:01,900 --> 00:39:04,990
and the B should
be on the circle.

693
00:39:04,990 --> 00:39:07,800
And the second step is
you draw another circle,

694
00:39:07,800 --> 00:39:10,020
and you try to find
the intersection

695
00:39:10,020 --> 00:39:12,180
and you connect everything.

696
00:39:12,180 --> 00:39:14,260
And then that's an
equilateral triangle.

697
00:39:14,260 --> 00:39:19,640
So there are only 5 or 6
steps in the formal of proof,

698
00:39:19,640 --> 00:39:23,180
and each step
corresponds perfectly

699
00:39:23,180 --> 00:39:26,410
to what's being done
in the informal proof.

700
00:39:26,410 --> 00:39:29,890
And we did this for
all the 48 theorems

701
00:39:29,890 --> 00:39:33,590
and proofs in Euclid's
Elements, Book I.

702
00:39:33,590 --> 00:39:37,490
And this is aesthetically
very pleasing for me,

703
00:39:37,490 --> 00:39:40,310
because to the best
of our knowledge,

704
00:39:40,310 --> 00:39:43,030
we are actually the
first to formalize

705
00:39:43,030 --> 00:39:47,470
Euclid's Elements in a
proof assistants like Lean.

706
00:39:47,470 --> 00:39:51,730
And while we want to keep
the proofs very faithful

707
00:39:51,730 --> 00:39:54,370
in the sense that there
is a direct correspondence

708
00:39:54,370 --> 00:39:56,690
between formal and informal.

709
00:39:56,690 --> 00:40:02,710
And as we will see later,
this is not always trivial.

710
00:40:02,710 --> 00:40:07,000
And during this process, we find
a few very interesting errors

711
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:09,160
in Euclid's Elements.

712
00:40:09,160 --> 00:40:12,170
I think we can probably go
over one of those errors,

713
00:40:12,170 --> 00:40:14,440
just to see how
formalization can

714
00:40:14,440 --> 00:40:18,340
help you check mathematical
proofs rigorously and uncover

715
00:40:18,340 --> 00:40:21,070
bugs in a proof
that you may think

716
00:40:21,070 --> 00:40:23,560
it's already very rigorous.

717
00:40:23,560 --> 00:40:28,300
So this is proposition
24 from Euclid's Book I.

718
00:40:28,300 --> 00:40:31,390
It says if you
have two triangles,

719
00:40:31,390 --> 00:40:37,390
and then the two
triangles have two pairs

720
00:40:37,390 --> 00:40:39,230
of edges equal to each other.

721
00:40:39,230 --> 00:40:47,060
So AC equals to DF and AB equals
to DE, so blue equals to blue,

722
00:40:47,060 --> 00:40:48,680
red to equals to red.

723
00:40:48,680 --> 00:40:53,080
And the angle A is
bigger than angle D.

724
00:40:53,080 --> 00:40:59,170
So you want to show that
BC is greater than EF.

725
00:40:59,170 --> 00:41:03,230
Well, this is doesn't
look very difficult,

726
00:41:03,230 --> 00:41:08,320
and I'm going to show the
original proof by Euclid's.

727
00:41:08,320 --> 00:41:12,460
So first you try to move
the triangle from the left

728
00:41:12,460 --> 00:41:19,400
to overlap with the triangle on
the right, so C will become G.

729
00:41:19,400 --> 00:41:23,300
And then you try
to connect F and G.

730
00:41:23,300 --> 00:41:31,190
So now the problem becomes
proving EF greater than EG.

731
00:41:31,190 --> 00:41:35,810
This is easier because EF and
EG are now in the same triangle.

732
00:41:35,810 --> 00:41:40,702
And according to a previously
proved theorem in the same book,

733
00:41:40,702 --> 00:41:44,360
in the same triangle,
a bigger angle

734
00:41:44,360 --> 00:41:46,920
corresponds to a bigger edge.

735
00:41:46,920 --> 00:41:52,160
So you only have to prove the
relationship between these two

736
00:41:52,160 --> 00:41:53,070
angles.

737
00:41:53,070 --> 00:41:56,840
You have to prove the big
angle here is indeed bigger

738
00:41:56,840 --> 00:42:00,530
than the small angle, so how?

739
00:42:00,530 --> 00:42:04,430
Just notice that we have
a isosceles triangle here.

740
00:42:04,430 --> 00:42:06,800
We have DGF.

741
00:42:06,800 --> 00:42:09,500
DG equals to DF.

742
00:42:09,500 --> 00:42:13,940
And by our previously proved
lemma, in an isosceles angle--

743
00:42:13,940 --> 00:42:15,890
in an isosceles
triangle, you have

744
00:42:15,890 --> 00:42:18,440
two angles equal to each other.

745
00:42:18,440 --> 00:42:22,910
So then you can
argue that the bigger

746
00:42:22,910 --> 00:42:29,900
angle is bigger than
the DFG because it's

747
00:42:29,900 --> 00:42:31,880
a superset of the BFG.

748
00:42:31,880 --> 00:42:35,060
And the DFG is equal to DGF.

749
00:42:35,060 --> 00:42:38,810
And the DGF is bigger
than the smaller angle.

750
00:42:38,810 --> 00:42:40,700
Then you have proof
the bigger angle

751
00:42:40,700 --> 00:42:42,450
is bigger than the small angle.

752
00:42:42,450 --> 00:42:47,750
OK, so this is very trivial,
but the proof is actually wrong.

753
00:42:47,750 --> 00:42:49,560
Anyone knows why?

754
00:42:49,560 --> 00:42:50,060
No?

755
00:42:50,060 --> 00:42:52,010
OK.

756
00:42:52,010 --> 00:42:54,750
Well, technically it's not
wrong for this diagram,

757
00:42:54,750 --> 00:42:58,010
but what if they look like this?

758
00:42:58,010 --> 00:43:01,130
So let me try to
replay the proof.

759
00:43:01,130 --> 00:43:05,240
First, move the triangle
from the left to the right.

760
00:43:05,240 --> 00:43:09,080
Second, connect G
and F. Now, we want

761
00:43:09,080 --> 00:43:15,000
to prove EF greater
than EG, and we

762
00:43:15,000 --> 00:43:17,650
want to use this
isosceles triangles alpha.

763
00:43:17,650 --> 00:43:20,590
So this alpha are equal.

764
00:43:20,590 --> 00:43:26,820
And we want to prove x greater
than y using alpha, which

765
00:43:26,820 --> 00:43:28,380
does not work directly.

766
00:43:28,380 --> 00:43:32,010
Because in the previous
diagram, alpha is a part of x.

767
00:43:32,010 --> 00:43:34,570
But now alpha is
outside x and y,

768
00:43:34,570 --> 00:43:37,630
so you cannot use it as an
angle in the middle anymore.

769
00:43:37,630 --> 00:43:41,610
So the original argument
break in this diagram.

770
00:43:41,610 --> 00:43:43,240
So how do we fix this proof?

771
00:43:43,240 --> 00:43:44,500
Well, luckily it's fixable.

772
00:43:44,500 --> 00:43:50,700
Otherwise, we will probably
publish a bigger paper.

773
00:43:50,700 --> 00:43:58,480
So first, notice that the
angle DGE, that's alpha plus y.

774
00:43:58,480 --> 00:44:00,640
It's the inner
angle in a triangle,

775
00:44:00,640 --> 00:44:02,080
so it's smaller than pi.

776
00:44:02,080 --> 00:44:03,070
That's trivial.

777
00:44:03,070 --> 00:44:07,300
And second, 2pi
minus alpha, minus x,

778
00:44:07,300 --> 00:44:11,020
that's DFE is also
smaller than pi.

779
00:44:11,020 --> 00:44:15,270
If you add these inequalities
together, alpha will cancel out

780
00:44:15,270 --> 00:44:17,880
and you get x greater than y.

781
00:44:17,880 --> 00:44:22,380
So it can be fixed, but as
you see this proof is quite

782
00:44:22,380 --> 00:44:24,330
different from Euclid's proof.

783
00:44:24,330 --> 00:44:27,790
So that's why we think Euclid's
has made an error here.

784
00:44:27,790 --> 00:44:29,950
Or technically
it's not an error.

785
00:44:29,950 --> 00:44:33,700
It's like this
proof has two cases.

786
00:44:33,700 --> 00:44:35,620
These two cases are
slightly different

787
00:44:35,620 --> 00:44:38,410
and the one case is more
challenging than the other.

788
00:44:38,410 --> 00:44:42,450
But Euclid's omitted the
more challenging case

789
00:44:42,450 --> 00:44:45,630
and focused on the easy case.

790
00:44:45,630 --> 00:44:48,580
OK, maybe that's a very
interesting digression.

791
00:44:48,580 --> 00:44:53,280
That's not related to AI,
but it showed how AI and Lean

792
00:44:53,280 --> 00:44:55,290
can make things more rigorous.

793
00:44:55,290 --> 00:44:58,710
So let's go back to AI.

794
00:44:58,710 --> 00:45:04,560
So in our benchmark, the
first thing good about it

795
00:45:04,560 --> 00:45:08,130
is it enables you to check
whether the autoformalized

796
00:45:08,130 --> 00:45:09,760
theorem statement is correct.

797
00:45:09,760 --> 00:45:12,700
So here is how we check if
the statement is correct.

798
00:45:12,700 --> 00:45:14,260
We have a ground
truth, and we want

799
00:45:14,260 --> 00:45:17,410
to check if it's logically-- if
the model-generated statement is

800
00:45:17,410 --> 00:45:20,080
logically equivalent
to the ground truth.

801
00:45:20,080 --> 00:45:23,210
So given two
theorems, T1 and T2,

802
00:45:23,210 --> 00:45:28,210
we check it by proving
T1 implies T2 and T2 also

803
00:45:28,210 --> 00:45:29,540
implies T1.

804
00:45:29,540 --> 00:45:32,290
And here we use an
automated reasoning

805
00:45:32,290 --> 00:45:35,140
tool called SMT solver.

806
00:45:35,140 --> 00:45:38,030
But if you remember I said
that this is infeasible,

807
00:45:38,030 --> 00:45:40,420
but how it becomes feasible now.

808
00:45:40,420 --> 00:45:43,180
And it turns out
now it's feasible

809
00:45:43,180 --> 00:45:46,660
because the theorems
in Euclidean geometry

810
00:45:46,660 --> 00:45:48,380
takes a particular form.

811
00:45:48,380 --> 00:45:50,620
They are not general theorems.

812
00:45:50,620 --> 00:45:53,650
So problems
intractable in general

813
00:45:53,650 --> 00:45:56,160
can be made tractable
in a specific domain.

814
00:45:58,870 --> 00:46:02,510
And so that's how we check
the theorem statement.

815
00:46:02,510 --> 00:46:06,050
Another problem is
autoformalizing the proofs.

816
00:46:06,050 --> 00:46:11,170
So here in Euclidean geometry,
we want to show that--

817
00:46:11,170 --> 00:46:14,890
we show that there
is a well known kind

818
00:46:14,890 --> 00:46:16,210
of gaps in the proofs.

819
00:46:16,210 --> 00:46:19,360
We call diagrammatic
reasoning gaps.

820
00:46:19,360 --> 00:46:21,260
I'm going to show
that using an example.

821
00:46:21,260 --> 00:46:24,670
So proposition one from
Euclid's Elements, Book I. Given

822
00:46:24,670 --> 00:46:27,400
two points, A and
B, you can construct

823
00:46:27,400 --> 00:46:29,860
a equilateral triangle.

824
00:46:29,860 --> 00:46:31,810
Here is Euclid's proof.

825
00:46:31,810 --> 00:46:36,460
Draw a circle centered at
A and B is on the circle.

826
00:46:36,460 --> 00:46:40,210
Draw another circle centered
at B, A is on the circle.

827
00:46:40,210 --> 00:46:43,850
Take the intersection, C,
and connect everything.

828
00:46:43,850 --> 00:46:46,090
You get an equilateral triangle.

829
00:46:46,090 --> 00:46:48,320
Anyone disagree with this proof?

830
00:46:48,320 --> 00:46:48,820
No?

831
00:46:48,820 --> 00:46:50,170
OK.

832
00:46:50,170 --> 00:46:54,430
The problem is, did we
prove C actually exists?

833
00:46:54,430 --> 00:46:59,110
Did we prove these two circles
actually have an intersection?

834
00:46:59,110 --> 00:47:00,260
We didn't.

835
00:47:00,260 --> 00:47:03,260
And actually if you try
to prove it, because--

836
00:47:03,260 --> 00:47:04,670
so this is proposition one.

837
00:47:04,670 --> 00:47:08,600
So to prove this proposition,
you can only use the axioms.

838
00:47:08,600 --> 00:47:10,550
There's no existing lemmas.

839
00:47:10,550 --> 00:47:16,340
And if you try to use Euclid's
axioms to try to prove these two

840
00:47:16,340 --> 00:47:20,570
circles intersect, you
cannot prove it, actually.

841
00:47:20,570 --> 00:47:23,360
So it's not provable.

842
00:47:23,360 --> 00:47:26,210
But no one bothers to
prove it because it's just

843
00:47:26,210 --> 00:47:28,250
so obvious in the diagram.

844
00:47:28,250 --> 00:47:31,130
But it becomes a problem
if I want to translate

845
00:47:31,130 --> 00:47:32,270
this proof in Lean.

846
00:47:32,270 --> 00:47:33,900
When I formalize
the proof in Lean,

847
00:47:33,900 --> 00:47:36,140
Lean does not know
what is the diagram

848
00:47:36,140 --> 00:47:38,810
or what is obvious
in the diagram.

849
00:47:38,810 --> 00:47:44,960
So I kind of have to formalize
what is obvious in the diagram.

850
00:47:44,960 --> 00:47:49,483
I was in the Zoom
in the last lecture,

851
00:47:49,483 --> 00:47:50,900
I believe someone
asked a question

852
00:47:50,900 --> 00:47:55,010
about how do we formalize
Euclidean geometry in Lean.

853
00:47:55,010 --> 00:47:58,070
I think this is very closely
related to that question.

854
00:47:58,070 --> 00:48:00,990
To formalize Euclidean
geometry in Lean,

855
00:48:00,990 --> 00:48:04,320
the most important thing, or
one of the most important thing,

856
00:48:04,320 --> 00:48:07,250
is how do you formalize
the concept of being

857
00:48:07,250 --> 00:48:09,650
obvious in the diagram?

858
00:48:09,650 --> 00:48:16,130
And luckily, this problem has
actually been solved before.

859
00:48:16,130 --> 00:48:23,690
So this is a work from Avigad
who is a professor in philosophy

860
00:48:23,690 --> 00:48:25,850
now in CMU.

861
00:48:25,850 --> 00:48:28,820
So Avigad essentially
identified a set

862
00:48:28,820 --> 00:48:32,790
of axioms that's not in
Euclid's axiom system.

863
00:48:32,790 --> 00:48:37,710
You can think of it as an
unspoken axiom or hidden axiom.

864
00:48:37,710 --> 00:48:42,030
And so what is being
obvious in the diagram,

865
00:48:42,030 --> 00:48:46,980
it is just if a fact is a
consequence of those axioms,

866
00:48:46,980 --> 00:48:49,610
then it's obvious
in the diagram.

867
00:48:49,610 --> 00:48:51,560
So now we have a
computational meaning

868
00:48:51,560 --> 00:48:53,900
of obviousness in
the diagram, and we

869
00:48:53,900 --> 00:48:57,380
can implement an automated
reasoning algorithm

870
00:48:57,380 --> 00:49:01,490
to try to derive things
that are obvious.

871
00:49:01,490 --> 00:49:04,230
So let me put
everything together.

872
00:49:04,230 --> 00:49:06,870
So in this work, we
have a benchmark.

873
00:49:06,870 --> 00:49:08,980
So in the benchmark
we have diagram.

874
00:49:08,980 --> 00:49:11,430
We have informal
math and we have

875
00:49:11,430 --> 00:49:14,830
a ground truth formal statement
that's written by human.

876
00:49:14,830 --> 00:49:19,150
So now, we use a model
to do autoformalization.

877
00:49:19,150 --> 00:49:22,980
It generates a formal
theorem, and we

878
00:49:22,980 --> 00:49:26,370
can check whether it's
equivalent to the ground truth

879
00:49:26,370 --> 00:49:31,830
using SMT solvers to prove
that they imply each other.

880
00:49:31,830 --> 00:49:34,540
So after checking the
statement equivalence,

881
00:49:34,540 --> 00:49:38,140
the model can also do
proof autoformalization.

882
00:49:38,140 --> 00:49:42,310
Say it generates this proof
and we give this proof to Lean.

883
00:49:42,310 --> 00:49:46,710
Lean will identify a set
of holes in the proof.

884
00:49:46,710 --> 00:49:51,010
And we give those
holes to SMT solver,

885
00:49:51,010 --> 00:49:55,720
which will tell us if the holes
are obvious in the diagram.

886
00:49:55,720 --> 00:49:58,780
And if everything
works, then we conclude,

887
00:49:58,780 --> 00:50:01,180
OK, this is a correct
formalization.

888
00:50:01,180 --> 00:50:03,900
So what our benchmark
really do here

889
00:50:03,900 --> 00:50:07,710
is we provide a
protocol that you

890
00:50:07,710 --> 00:50:12,720
can use to rigorously
evaluate autoformalization.

891
00:50:12,720 --> 00:50:16,330
And so after collecting
this benchmark,

892
00:50:16,330 --> 00:50:19,140
we also did some very
simple experiments

893
00:50:19,140 --> 00:50:22,810
using just GPT-4 and
the GPT-4 Vision.

894
00:50:22,810 --> 00:50:25,030
And here we don't
train the model.

895
00:50:25,030 --> 00:50:28,470
We just give a few
demonstrations to the model

896
00:50:28,470 --> 00:50:32,310
and ask it to autoformalize
the theorem statement.

897
00:50:32,310 --> 00:50:35,910
So blue here is
GPT-4 and green is

898
00:50:35,910 --> 00:50:39,730
GPT-4 V, which also takes
the diagram as input.

899
00:50:39,730 --> 00:50:42,310
And so here are two takeaways.

900
00:50:42,310 --> 00:50:47,490
One is if you look at
the x-axis, is how many

901
00:50:47,490 --> 00:50:49,630
demonstrations you
give to the model.

902
00:50:49,630 --> 00:50:51,750
So if you give the model
more demonstrations,

903
00:50:51,750 --> 00:50:53,640
it's going to do better.

904
00:50:53,640 --> 00:50:58,110
But even if you give 5
demonstrations or even more,

905
00:50:58,110 --> 00:51:02,520
the model get only about
20% which is pretty low,

906
00:51:02,520 --> 00:51:06,430
which shows autoformalization
is very challenging.

907
00:51:06,430 --> 00:51:08,810
And if you compare
green with blue,

908
00:51:08,810 --> 00:51:11,200
you see green is a
little bit better.

909
00:51:11,200 --> 00:51:15,340
Which shows if you give
the diagram to the model,

910
00:51:15,340 --> 00:51:20,200
it has a stronger understanding
of the geometry problem.

911
00:51:20,200 --> 00:51:22,450
And here are some takeaways.

912
00:51:22,450 --> 00:51:25,570
We identified two challenges
in autoformalization.

913
00:51:25,570 --> 00:51:28,900
For theorem statement, you
don't know how to evaluate.

914
00:51:28,900 --> 00:51:31,540
For the proofs, there
are too many holes

915
00:51:31,540 --> 00:51:33,250
in the human written proofs.

916
00:51:33,250 --> 00:51:37,880
And we address them by
limiting to a specific domain.

917
00:51:37,880 --> 00:51:41,980
So in this domain, we can
build a equivalence checker to

918
00:51:41,980 --> 00:51:43,130
for the statement.

919
00:51:43,130 --> 00:51:48,580
And we can build a tool that
can fill in all the gaps.

920
00:51:48,580 --> 00:51:52,000
But still the open problem
is really how do you

921
00:51:52,000 --> 00:51:57,280
generalize across a broader
set of mathematical domains?

922
00:51:57,280 --> 00:52:02,290
So now we've covered both task,
autoformalization and theorem

923
00:52:02,290 --> 00:52:03,400
proving.

924
00:52:03,400 --> 00:52:05,260
And this is the end
of this lecture,

925
00:52:05,260 --> 00:52:08,100
and I'm happy to take questions.

